e wider culture The phrase “our physically disabled staff end up in” suggests a pattern where the organization might be failing disabled employees. This isn’t just a one-off invite mistake; it hints at a culture that sees disabled colleagues as less capable or harder to accommodate. If you’re in a leadership or coordinating role, it’s worth examining whether there are systemic accessibility issues that need fixing—not just for this teammate but for any current or future disabled staff. That might mean reviewing meeting practices, physical spaces, communication tools, and training on disability inclusion. **In short, you don’t “brief him afterwards” as if you did him a favor. You own the harm, apologize, share the missed information, and immediately change your approach so that he never again has to be briefed instead of being present.** The most professional, respectful thing you can do now is publicly correct your mistake by including him in the next call and ensuring the environment actually works for him.
